# Decorative roof over the walk-through St. Gilgen houses (NON-solid, like
# wall_village_bg; never standable): a shaded terracotta Biberschwanz roof,
# n tiles with R joints and rounded R tile bottoms, no light lip anywhere
# (art-style 8: only solid tiles get a light top edge). Sets it apart from
# the solid roof_shingle (brown O/l wood shingles with an o lip).
# Autotile convention (D-020 mask; 1 = same tile above, 2 = right,
# 4 = below, 8 = left; level edge = same):
#   left open (no 8)  -> left slope: 45 deg R verge from the bottom-left corner
#                        to the top-right corner, sky above it;
#   right open (no 2) -> right slope: the mirror, with a 2 px R shaded verge;
#   both closed       -> full roof (ridge part); both open -> small pyramid;
#   top open (no 1)   -> ridge cap: R line + l shadow on rows 0-1;
#   bottom open (no 4)-> eave: R + l rows 14-15 over the wall's i eave line.
# One row over a house = a trapezoid (slope / ridge ... / slope). Stack rows,
# each row one tile narrower on both sides, for a full gable: a 6-wide house
# gets 6 + 4 + 2 tiles and the slopes join into one 45 deg triangle. The run
# sits directly on the top wall_village_bg row (same width or one tile wider
# on each side for an overhang). Generated by the pixel-artist with a
# throwaway script; the grids below are the source.
sprite = tile_roof_bg
size   = 16x16
origin = 0,0
anim   = auto frames=16 ticks=1
